<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huvishka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 150–190 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huvishka was a Kushan emperor who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> and reigned for about three decades in the middle of the 2nd century CE, when the empire stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> to the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. His abundant gold coinage shows the widest range of gods of any Kushan ruler, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> flourished as a centre of art and religion under his rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huvishka.jpg" alt="Huvishka" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HuvishkaBMCoin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Huvishka was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> and succeeded him, on the best evidence available in 150 CE, ruling until the succession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva-i">Vasudeva I</a> about thirty years later. He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, but his reign encompassed many surrounding regions. His territory reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, places where he is known to have minted coins. Gold coins and amulets bearing his image have been found as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, one of them as an offering under the Enlightenment Throne of the Buddha, which suggests with other finds that Kushan rule may have reached that far east.</p><p>His reign seems to have been essentially peaceful, a period of consolidation that moved the centre of the empire to the southern capital city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. It is also known as the golden age of Kushan rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Huvishka supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, although the Buddha does not appear on his coinage, possibly out of respect. A statue found at Govindo-Nagar and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-museum-mathura">Mathura Museum</a>, dated to the twenty-eighth year of his reign, carries the first known epigraphic evidence of the Buddha Amitabha. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> manuscript fragment in the Schøyen Collection describes him as one who had set forth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a>.</p><p>His coins show a wide range of deities. Iranian gods include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atar">Atar</a>, and an inscription at Ayrtam near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/termez">Termez</a> records a temple to the deities <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharro">Pharro</a> and Ardoxsho that he founded between 164 and 174. Indian gods include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Skanda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and he restored a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, where provision was made for hospitality towards Brahmans. One coin shows the Hellenistic-Egyptian Serapis, which suggests an orientation towards Roman Egypt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Huvishka minted more gold coins than all other Kushan rulers combined, in a great variety of designs, mainly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, with smaller mints in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. Early in his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coinage fell in weight from a standard of 16 grams to about 10 or 11 grams and kept declining, until a standard coin weighed only 9 grams at the start of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva">Vasudeva</a>. The devaluation led to a large production of imitations, and its motives remain unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huvishka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
